/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Take the prestress P with eccentricity e(x) below the centroid.
   - In a continuous beam the primary moment tries to bend the beam into a curve the supports do not allow.
   - ep is the eccentricity of the resultant concrete compression below the centroid in mm (negative means above); e is the tendon eccentricity; Ms is the secondary moment, sagging positive, in N·mm; P in N.
   - A cable is concordant when Ms = 0 everywhere, so the pressure line coincides with the tendon.
   - Move the tendon at the intermediate supports up or down, keep its eccentricity at the two end supports, and keep its shape (curvature) inside every span.
   - A straight-line change has no curvature, so the equivalent load of the tendon inside each span is unchanged.
   - Two practical results follow.
   - A straight tendon at constant eccentricity e in a two-span beam is the standard exam case, and it is not concordant.
*/
