/* book Worked example: Q: A floor beam 300 mm × 650 mm of M40 concrete has four 12.7 mm strands (98.7 mm² each, fp = 1860 N/mm², effective stress 1116 N/mm²) at dp = 550 mm, and three 16 mm Fe 500 bars at ds = 600 mm. The service moment is 260 kN·m. Find (a) the degree of prestressing and the bottom stress at service, (b) the ultimate moment and the PPR, and (c) the number of strands full prestressing would need at the same eccentricity.
   1. (a) A = 195,000 mm²; Zb = 300 × 650²/6 = 21.125 × 10⁶ mm³; e = 550 − 325 = 225 mm. Pe = 4 × 98.7 × 1116 = 440.6 kN.
   2. Mdec = Pe(Zb/A + e) = 440,597 × (108.33 + 225) = 146.9 kN·m; λ = 146.9/260 = 0.56.
   3. Bottom stress at service = 2.259 + 4.693 − 12.308 = −5.36 N/mm², a tension of 5.36 N/mm² against fcr = 0.7√40 = 4.43: the beam cracks under full load, so it is Type 3 and the crack width must be checked.
   4. (b) Tendon force at ultimate = 394.8 × 0.87 × 1860 = 638,865 N; bar force = 603.2 × 0.87 × 500 = 262,386 N; total 901,251 N.
   5. xu = 901,251/(0.36 × 40 × 300) = 208.6 mm. Strains: tendon 0.0115 (well past elastic); bars 0.0035 × (391.4/208.6) = 0.0066 ≥ 0.00417, so both steels yield.
   6. Mu = 638,865 × (550 − 0.42 × 208.6) + 262,386 × (600 − 0.42 × 208.6) = 429.8 kN·m. PPR = 638,865/901,251 = 0.71.
   7. (c) Zero tension at 260 kN·m needs Pe ≥ 260 × 10⁶/(108.33 + 225) = 780 kN, that is 780/110.15 = 7.08 → 8 strands.
   Answer: λ = 0.56: the beam decompresses at 147 kN·m and has 5.36 N/mm² tension at 260 kN·m, so it is partially prestressed (Type 3). Mu = 430 kN·m with PPR = 0.71. Full prestressing would need 8 strands instead of 4, twice the prestressing steel, with a much larger camber under the permanent load. */
