/* book exam traps: Calling partial prestressing a lower grade of full prestressing. It is a deliberate choice, with its own checks for crack width and fatigue. | Adding the tendon and bar moments without checking that both steels have yielded. Use strain compatibility; the lever arms are different. | Using the gross section for stresses after the beam has cracked. Crack width and stiffness need the cracked section. | Forgetting losses in the service check: the degree of prestressing uses Pe, not the jacking force. | Mixing up the two ratios: λ is a service quantity (moments), PPR an ultimate one (steel forces). */
